Supreme People's Court

Opinion on Regulating and Strengthening the Work of Handling Pre-Litigation Preservation Cases

Fa [2024] No. 42

To the High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region; and all units of this Court:

To thoroughly implement fairness and efficiency, protect the lawful rights and interests of parties, and promote the governance of enforcement sources, in accordance with the provisions of relevant laws and judicial interpretations, this Opinion is formulated to regulate and strengthen the work of handling pre-litigation preservation cases.

I. General Provisions

Article 1 Regulating and strengthening the work of handling pre-litigation preservation cases is an inevitable requirement to meet the diverse judicial needs of the people, promote the simplified and expedited handling of civil cases, and build a fair, efficient, and authoritative socialist judicial system. People's courts shall strengthen the awareness of "as if I were in litigation," accurately apply the pre-litigation preservation system provided for in the Civil Procedure Law, protect the lawful rights and interests of parties in emergency situations, serve the work of "effectively solving the difficulty of enforcement," and lawfully ensure that the prevailing party realizes its rights in a timely manner.

People's courts shall strengthen the concept of "grasping the front end and treating the disease before it occurs," implement the principle of voluntary mediation and priority of mediation when handling pre-litigation preservation cases, promote the organic connection of pre-litigation preservation, case registration and filing, and the integration of litigation and mediation, strive to "promote mediation through preservation" and "promote enforcement through preservation," comprehensively enhance the ability to substantively resolve disputes, and achieve case closure and social harmony.

Article 2 The term "pre-litigation preservation" as used in this Opinion includes property preservation, evidence preservation, and conduct preservation adopted by a people's court upon application by an interested party in accordance with the provisions of Articles 84 and 104 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China.

The term "applicant" as used in this Opinion refers to the "interested party" as provided for in Articles 84 and 104 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China.

II. Acceptance of Applications

Article 3 For a pre-litigation preservation application submitted by an applicant, the people's court at the location of the property or evidence to be preserved, the domicile of the person against whom the application is made, or the court having jurisdiction over the case shall not refuse to accept the application on the grounds that pre-litigation preservation is inconvenient to implement or that litigation preservation can only be applied for upon registration and filing of the lawsuit.

Article 4 Where the pre-litigation preservation materials submitted offline by the applicant do not meet the requirements, the people's court shall, on the spot and at one time, inform the applicant of the need to supplement them. After the applicant supplements the materials, the people's court must, within forty-eight hours from the time of receiving the supplemented materials, render a ruling on whether to grant the application.

Where the applicant submits pre-litigation preservation materials online during non-working hours, the people's court shall calculate the time period from the beginning of the first working day after receiving the pre-litigation preservation application.

Article 5 Where the applicant simultaneously applies for pre-litigation conduct preservation, an injunction against infringement of personality rights, or a personal safety protection order based on the same facts and reasons, or if the application is unclear, the people's court shall explain the functional positioning and applicable circumstances of the three legal systems and guide the applicant to choose the legal system more favorable to protecting their own lawful rights and interests.

Article 6 Where an applicant applies for pre-litigation preservation, the people's court shall inform the applicant of the legal consequence that if the applicant fails to file a lawsuit or apply for arbitration in accordance with the law within thirty days after the preservation measures are taken, the preservation will be lifted.

Article 7 Before accepting a pre-litigation preservation case, the people's court shall examine whether the security provided by the party complies with the provisions of the law. Where an applicant applies for pre-litigation property preservation, the applicant shall provide security equivalent to the amount of the preservation sought, except in cases of urgency and special circumstances.

For applications for pre-litigation evidence preservation or conduct preservation, the amount of security shall be determined by the people's court based on the specific circumstances.

Article 8 Where an applicant applies for pre-litigation property preservation and falls under any of the following circumstances, the people's court may, as it deems appropriate, determine the amount of security:

(1) Claims for alimony, child support, education expenses, pension, medical expenses, labor remuneration, or work-related injury compensation;
(2) Cases of economic difficulty in marriage and family disputes;
(3) Claims for damages due to injuries suffered in acts of courage;
(4) Other circumstances where the amount of security may be determined as appropriate.

Article 9 Where an applicant applies for pre-litigation conduct preservation and falls under any of the following circumstances, the people's court may, as it deems appropriate, determine the amount of security:

(1) Applying for pre-litigation conduct preservation in marriage and family disputes;
(2) Applying for conduct preservation because personality rights are being or are about to be infringed upon;
(3) Other circumstances where the amount of security may be determined as appropriate.

Article 10 Where an applicant applying for pre-litigation property preservation provides information on the property to be preserved that meets any of the following circumstances, the people's court may deem it as clear information on the property to be preserved:

(1) If the property is real estate, providing copies of the property certificate, title inquiry sheet, or other ownership certification materials, or specific information such as the name of the owner, property certificate number or pre-sale online contract number, administrative region, road, building name, specific room number, etc.;
(2) If the property is bank deposits, providing specific deposit information such as the depositor's name, name of the bank, and account number;
(3) If the property is a motor vehicle, providing specific information such as information on the vehicle custodian or controller, license plate number, and vehicle registration authority; if requesting seizure, providing the specific parking location of the motor vehicle;
(4) If the property is equity in a limited liability company or a non-listed joint stock limited company, providing information such as the specific company name, unified social credit code, and registration (or custody) institution, capital contribution amount, and shareholding percentage; if the property is listed company stocks or other seizable marketable securities, providing corresponding account information and the name and address of the trading venue or securities company;
(5) If the property is an accrued claim, providing information such as the creditor's name, debtor's name and domicile, amount of the claim, maturity date of the claim, claim certificate or relevant supporting materials;
(6) If the property is government bonds or funds, providing the name, type, quantity, and registration authority of the government bonds or funds;
(7) If the property is intellectual property rights such as patent rights, trademark rights, or copyrights, providing the registration number of the right certificate or other ownership certification;
(8) If the property is other property, providing detailed information such as the name, type, specifications, quantity, value, owner, and specific location of the property, as well as relevant evidentiary materials.

III. Granting Pre-Litigation Preservation Applications

Article 11 Where a person against whom a pre-litigation property preservation application is made falls under any of the following circumstances, causing or possibly causing the loss of the ability to perform debts, the people's court may deem it as an urgent circumstance:

(1) Acts of transferring, concealing, or selling property;
(2) Acts such as capital flight to evade debt performance;
(3) Severe deterioration of production and business conditions;
(4) Loss of commercial credibility;
(5) Being listed as a dishonest person subject to enforcement;
(6) Other circumstances causing or possibly causing the loss of the ability to perform debts.

Article 12 Where an application for pre-litigation conduct preservation falls under any of the following circumstances, the people's court may deem it as an urgent circumstance:

(1) The applicant's personal rights and interests are facing or are about to face the danger of unlawful infringement;
(2) The applicant's property rights and interests are facing or are about to face the danger of unlawful infringement;
(3) The conduct of the person against whom the application is made would render the infringement difficult to control and likely increase the damage to the applicant;
(4) Other circumstances where the applicant's rights and interests are facing or are about to cause irreparable damage.

Article 13 When examining an application for pre-litigation conduct preservation, the people's court shall comprehensively consider the following factors:

(1) Whether the applicant's request has a factual basis and legal support;
(2) Whether failing to take pre-litigation conduct preservation measures would cause irreparable damage to the applicant's lawful rights and interests;
(3) Whether the damage to the applicant from not taking pre-litigation conduct preservation measures would exceed the damage to the person against whom the application is made from taking conduct preservation measures;
(4) The possible impact of taking pre-litigation conduct preservation measures on national interests and social public interests;
(5) Other factors that should be considered.

Article 14 Where the applicant simultaneously applies for property preservation, conduct preservation, and evidence preservation, the people's court shall render a single ruling on whether to grant the application; if there are circumstances affecting the implementation of preservation measures, separate rulings may be rendered.

IV. Taking Pre-Litigation Preservation Measures

Article 15 The following property shall not be subject to pre-litigation property preservation measures by the people's court:

(1) Items and expenses necessary for the daily life, education, and medical care of the person against whom the application is made and their dependents and fosterlings;
(2) Special accounts for migrant worker wages and wage deposits, except as otherwise provided by law and where the lawsuit requests payment of migrant worker wages for the corresponding project under the special account;
(3) Deposit reserves and settlement funds deposited by financial institutions with the People's Bank;
(4) Special Renminbi deposit accounts for trust property;
(5) Social insurance fund accounts opened by social insurance institutions;
(6) Trading margin of securities operating institutions and futures brokerage institutions, letter of credit opening margin, and independent guarantee margin, except where such margin has lost its function;
(7) Special funds of trade unions and other social organizations;
(8) Property of a debtor for whom the people's court has ruled to accept a bankruptcy application;
(9) Educational facilities, medical and health facilities, elderly care service facilities, and other public welfare facilities of non-profit legal persons established for public welfare purposes, such as schools, kindergartens, medical institutions, and elderly care institutions;
(10) Property used for epidemic prevention and control, emergency response, rescue, and other tasks undertaking epidemic prevention and control or emergency response;
(11) Other property that shall not be sealed up, detained, or frozen as provided by laws, administrative regulations, or judicial interpretations.

Article 16 Where the purpose of preservation can be achieved, the people's court shall choose property that has a lesser impact on the life and production or business activities of the person against whom the application is made for preservation.

For property used in daily life and production or business activities that is placed in the custody of the person against whom the application is made as designated by the people's court, if continued use does not significantly affect the value of the property, the person against whom the application is made may be allowed to continue using it.

Article 17 The value of the property preserved upon approval of pre-litigation preservation shall be commensurate with the amount sought by the applicant for preservation, and shall not obviously exceed the subject matter or scope of preservation.

If it is discovered that the preservation obviously exceeds the subject matter, the people's court shall, upon the application of a party or ex officio, promptly lift the part of the preservation that obviously exceeds the subject matter, except where the preserved property is indivisible and the person against whom preservation is sought has no other property available for preservation, or where the security provided for replacement property is insufficient for full preservation.

Article 18 Where the people's court conducts pre-litigation preservation of electronic evidence, it shall simultaneously preserve necessary information for verifying the reliability of the preserved evidence, such as the source, destination, sending and receiving times, software operating environment, and operating system of the electronic information, and may seize storage media such as relevant computer hosts, hard drives, and servers.

Article 19 Where the people's court rules to take preservation measures, it shall serve the ruling on the applicant and the person against whom the application is made. If serving the ruling on the person against whom the application is made may affect the implementation of the preservation measures, the people's court shall serve the ruling on the person against whom the application is made within no more than forty-eight hours from the time the preservation measures are taken.

Article 20 Where any of the following circumstances exists, the people's court shall promptly lift the pre-litigation preservation:

(1) The people's court discovers that there is an error in the preservation;
(2) The applicant applies to lift the preservation, or the person against whom the application is made applies to lift the preservation and the applicant consents;
(3) The applicant fails to file a lawsuit or apply for arbitration in accordance with the law within thirty days after the people's court takes the preservation measures;
(4) The person against whom pre-litigation property preservation measures have been taken has entered bankruptcy proceedings as a debtor;
(5) Other circumstances as provided by laws, administrative regulations, or judicial interpretations for lifting pre-litigation preservation.

V. Improving Supporting Coordination Mechanisms

Article 21 Where an applicant, a person against whom the application is made, or another person disrupts the order of pre-litigation preservation, the people's court may, in accordance with Articles 114 and 115 of the Civil Procedure Law, impose a fine or detention depending on the severity of the circumstances; if the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law.

Article 22 People's courts shall, by publishing guiding cases, clarify the specific circumstances and legal consequences of an erroneous application, guiding applicants to apply for pre-litigation preservation lawfully and rationally.

Article 23 In the process of building a "single network," people's courts shall correlate the information of the applicant's pre-litigation preservation application and the filing of the lawsuit, achieving interconnection, intercommunication, and real-time sharing.

Article 24 Where the pre-litigation preservation court discovers that the applicant has filed a lawsuit with the people's court having jurisdiction over the case, it shall communicate and coordinate with the people's court having jurisdiction over the case and transfer the preservation procedures. After the pre-litigation preservation procedures are transferred, the pre-litigation preservation ruling shall be deemed to have been rendered by the receiving people's court.

Article 25 The case filing department of the people's court shall be responsible for ruling on whether to grant pre-litigation preservation and for taking conduct preservation and evidence preservation measures; the enforcement department shall be responsible for taking pre-litigation property preservation measures. People's courts shall strengthen trial management, orderly coordinate the work of the case filing and enforcement departments for pre-litigation preservation, and ensure that execution begins immediately after a ruling to take pre-litigation preservation measures.

Article 26 If the people's court that took the pre-litigation preservation measures does not have jurisdiction over the case, it may, with the consent of both parties, organize pre-litigation mediation and guide the resolution of the dispute through non-litigation methods.

Article 27 People's courts shall optimize assessment indicators, explore the establishment of an assessment and incentive mechanism with the pre-litigation preservation implementation rate, preservation success rate, dispute resolution rate, and enforcement realization rate as the main content, and guide judicial personnel to accurately apply the pre-litigation preservation system in accordance with the law.

VI. Supplementary Provisions

Article 28 This Opinion shall be interpreted by the Supreme People's Court. Each High People's Court may, in accordance with relevant laws, judicial interpretations, and this Opinion, and in light of the actual trial and enforcement work of its region, formulate or revise implementing rules on pre-litigation preservation, which shall be reported to the Supreme People's Court for filing.

Article 29 This Opinion shall come into force on March 1, 2024.

February 7, 2024
